The Nautilus DevOps team needs to set up a new EC2 instance that can be accessed securely from their landing host (aws-client). The instance should be of type t2.micro and named xfusion-ec2. A new SSH key with name id_rsa should be created on the aws-client host under the/root/.ssh/ folder, if it doesn't already exist. This key should then be added to the root user's authorised keys on the EC2 instance, allowing passwordless SSH access from the aws-client host.

ChatGPT said:
To complete this task, perform the following steps.

1. Create the SSH key on aws-client
Log in to the aws-client host and check whether the key already exists:

ls -l /root/.ssh/id_rsa /root/.ssh/id_rsa.pub

If it does not exist, create it:

ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 2048 -f /root/.ssh/id_rsa -N ""

This creates:

/root/.ssh/id_rsa
/root/.ssh/id_rsa.pub
2. Launch the EC2 instance
In the AWS EC2 Console (Region: us-east-1):

Launch a new instance.
Name: xfusion-ec2
AMI: Any Linux AMI (e.g. Ubuntu or Amazon Linux)
Instance type: t2.micro
Wait until:

Instance state = Running
2/2 Status checks passed
3. Add the public key to the EC2 instance
Display the public key on aws-client:

cat /root/.ssh/id_rsa.pub

Copy the entire output.

SSH into the EC2 instance using the default user (for example ubuntu or ec2-user), then become root:

sudo -i

Create the .ssh directory if needed:

mkdir -p /root/.ssh
chmod 700 /root/.ssh

Append the copied public key:

echo "PASTE_THE_CONTENTS_OF_id_rsa.pub_HERE" >> /root/.ssh/authorized_keys
chmod 600 /root/.ssh/authorized_keys

4. Verify passwordless SSH
From the aws-client host, test the connection:

ssh -i /root/.ssh/id_rsa root@<EC2_PUBLIC_IP>

If you log in without being prompted for a password, the setup is complete.

This satisfies the requirements:

EC2 instance named xfusion-ec2
Instance type t2.micro
SSH key /root/.ssh/id_rsa created on aws-client if it didn't already exist
Public key added to /root/.ssh/authorized_keys on the EC2 instance for passwordless root access.
